# Doctor global activation — the activation window

Extends rules 153, 154, 156 and **157**, which it does not replace. Rule 157 owns
the governance phase and the attested precondition; this one owns what the
Phase-0 delta recheck measured about **entering an activation window**, and what
the Half-A cutover proved when one was finally entered.

> ## STATUS — SINGLE SESSION LIVE, BRANCH LOCK OFF (2026-09-17)
>
> ```
> single_active_session = TRUE       <- lease engine live, all 15 doctors
> branch_lock           = FALSE      <- disarmed by owner decision, 22:5x WITA
> BRANCH_LOCK_EFFECTIVE = FALSE      <- no branch narrowing
> session probe observable = TRUE
> HALF B = INACTIVE   scope pilot [9,15,18] · global_permitted false
> ```
>
> **The business model is: a doctor may work at ANY branch, provided they log in
> on a registered device of that branch.** A home lock pinning a doctor to one
> branch is the wrong instrument for that model, so the capability is off while
> all 15 lock rows are retained.
>
> Branch lock WAS live for ~40 minutes that night and was **proven to work**
> (AW-R14). It is off because the business does not want that behaviour — not
> because it failed. Re-arming is one flag plus a cache rebuild.
>
> **Half of the stated model is not yet enforced.** "Must log in on a registered
> device" currently binds only the 3-doctor cohort `[9,15,18]`; the other 12 can
> still log in by browser with no device. Making it fleet-wide **is Half B**,
> which remains blocked (AW-R5..AW-R7). Do not describe the model as fully
> enforced.

## AW-R1 — a recheck GO is not an APPLY approval

`doctor-access-global-activation-phase0-delta-recheck-1-go` means the prerequisites
reconcile, the mechanics are re-derived and the window plan is executable. It means
**`GLOBAL_ACTIVATION_APPLY_AUTHORIZED = NO`**. The next step is a separate human
decision — `APPROVE_DOCTOR_GLOBAL_ACTIVATION_APPLY=YES/NO`. Never fold the owner's
approval into a tag, and never report `GLOBAL_ACTIVATION_GO=YES` or
`GLOBAL_ENFORCEMENT_GO=YES` from a readiness sprint.

## AW-R2 — Half A is fleet-wide and non-staged

`FEATURE_DOCTOR_SINGLE_ACTIVE_SESSION=true` is a **cutover**, not a pilot, a wave
or gradual enablement. `DoctorSessionLeaseService::subjectTo()` is
`$user->hasRole('Doctor')` and nothing else; the listener is registered
unconditionally and the middleware sits in the **global `web` group**. There is no
cohort, no cap and no staged mechanism — do not describe one unless source grows one.

## AW-R3 — the two effects land at different moments, and ride different flags

This is the correction an adversarial review earned, sharpened by the night the
two flags were finally set independently.

- **Branch narrowing is immediate and fleet-wide — but only when BOTH flags are
  on.** `RmeWorkingBranchScope` and `ClinicVisitService` call `branchIdFor()` on
  every request, so the instant `DoctorEffectiveBranchResolver::enabled()` flips
  true, an already-logged-in doctor's lists narrow and cross-branch writes are
  refused. With `branch_lock` false that predicate is false and **nothing
  narrows**, however live the lease engine is.
- **Lease enforcement begins at each doctor's NEXT login, and rides
  `single_active_session` alone.** A session holding no lease token passes through
  `EnsureDoctorSessionLease` forever, and leases mint only on the `Login` event.
  `DoctorSessionLeaseService::enabled()` does **not** consult `branch_lock`.

So the two halves of "Half A" are separable in production, and today they are
separated: **single session ON, branch narrowing OFF.** Never state one from the
other. Arming the lease evicts nobody; arming the branch lock narrows everybody at
once and still evicts nobody.

## AW-R4 — the lease population is WIDER than the branch-lock population

`DoctorEffectiveBranchResolver` gates on `hasRole('Doctor')` **AND NOT exempt**
(Owner / Super Admin / Supervisor RME). `subjectTo()` carries no exemption. A
governance account holding the Doctor role **is** subject to the session lease
while being exempt from the branch lock. Check both populations separately.

## AW-R5 — scoped enforcement is not global, and a big cohort is still `pilot`

`MODE_PILOT` covers `in_array($userId, cohort)`; `MODE_UNSCOPED` covers everyone.
Fleet-wide is live only when **unscoped mode AND `globalPermitted()` AND the flag
armed** are all true. A cohort naming many doctors satisfies none of them.

Enumeration fails **closed**: a cohort larger than `pilot_cohort_maximum` resolves
to `ids => []`, so it covers **NOBODY, not everybody**, and trips
`pilot_cohort_exceeds_reviewed_maximum`.

## AW-R6 — `global_permitted` is unreachable from a host, but not tamper-evident

It is **not reachable from an environment variable, a database row, or a runtime
write** — `config/doctor_device_enforcement.php` has no such key and there is no
`config()->set` anywhere in the application.

State it that way and no stronger. `globalPermitted()` reads `config()`, which
resolves from the **deployed file or the config cache**, not from git, and the
auditor reads the same value — so a host-side edit arms global enforcement and the
gate reports it consistently. **There is no drift detection against the committed
value.** "Requires a reviewed source-control change" is a deployment-integrity
property, not a code-enforced one.

## AW-R7 — four of five global prerequisites cannot be contradicted

`global_prerequisites_attested` is consumed by `Phase4aPilotPreparationScanner`,
which reads **only the attestation** and no measurement. The contradiction detector
lives in a different engine (`DoctorEstateResilienceVerdict`, surfaced by
`doctor:estate-resilience`) and covers exactly one of the five.

So signing `spare_device_available_per_branch => true` would **PASS the phase gate**
despite the measured FAIL, and `rollback_to_browser_login_proven` had **no producer
anywhere under `app/`** — nothing could contradict it at all. Extending measurement
and contradiction detection to the remaining prerequisites is a prerequisite of
Half B, not a nicety.

