## AW-R8 — lease cleanup belongs INSIDE the window

A stale unreleased lease is dead only while its user has no live session row —
`IncumbentSessionProbe::isLive()` keys on `sessions.user_id`, not on the lease's
recorded `session_id`. A login taken **while the flag is off** writes a session row
and claims no lease, so arming afterwards turns that lease into a live incumbent
that refuses the next login.

Therefore: release stale leases **immediately before the cutover**, never days
earlier — a lease released today is replaced the moment that doctor logs in again.
Use `doctor:session-force-logout` (dry run first); it carries no flag guard, which
is precisely what makes cleanup possible before Half A arms.

## AW-R9 — `online = false` is not proof of a clean window

The presence table can hold an open row for a session that no longer exists. The
clinical hard gate is all of: zero live doctor sessions in `sessions` within
`SESSION_LIFETIME`; zero doctors holding a `clinic_room_id` with `offline_at IS NULL`;
zero visits `in_progress`; zero visits dated today (or each explicitly accepted);
`isLive()` false for every unreleased lease; and no approved cover overlapping the
window. Measure it within minutes of the cutover, never hours.

## AW-R10 — capture the scope, restore the capture

The pilot cohort is the **union of two host variables** — a singular
`..._DOCTOR_USER_ID` and a plural `..._DOCTOR_USER_IDS`. A rollback restoring only
one restores the wrong cohort. Capture with `android:phase4a-pilot-scope --json`,
which reports the resolved union, and restore **that**, never a remembered constant.

Know one reporting subtlety before an incident: `declared_pilot_doctor_user_id`
reads `null` whenever the resolved cohort has more than one member. That is by
design and is **not** a sign the singular variable is unset.

## AW-R11 — fetch before you judge ancestry

A stale local branch ref will report every prerequisite GO tag as *not* an ancestor
of the base branch, which is false. Fetch first and resolve against `origin/…` or
against the production head itself.

## AW-R12 — flags off does not mean nothing writes

True of the per-request and per-login hot path, which returns before any query.
**False of the subsystem**: `DoctorSessionReleaseService`, `DoctorBranchLockApprovalService`
and `DoctorBranchLockBulkAssignmentService` all write locks, lease releases,
presence rows and `sys_audit_logs` rows with both flags off, by design and through
governed CLI commands. Do not claim blanket write-inertness; claim it about the
request path, which is where it is true.

## AW-R13 — deny-the-second, never evict-the-first (proven live)

Proven on production 2026-09-17, not argued. With a doctor holding a live session,
a **fully valid** second authorization on an **active** device
(`{"status":"active","doctor_id":20,"device_status":"active","doctor_device_id":3}`)
was refused with `{"reason": "active_session_elsewhere"}` — and her first session
and lease were **untouched**.

Never "fix" a lockout by making the second login evict the first. The clinician
already seeing patients keeps working; the person arriving second is told where
the live session is.

## AW-R14 — what branch lock does WHEN ARMED (proven live, now disarmed)

Same ceremony: a doctor locked to **TLK1** logged in on a tablet sitting
physically at **ATG3**, with no cover, and her effective branch resolved to
**TLK1**. The lease recorded `effective_branch_id = 1 (TLK1)`.

**Read this as a proof of the capability, not as current behaviour.**
`branch_lock` was set false the same night, so today the resolver is inert and a
doctor's working context follows where they log in — the model the owner chose:
*work at any branch, on a registered device of that branch.*

The durable half of this rule is the distinction, which holds in both postures:
**a device's branch is operational topology, never an access grant.** When branch
lock is armed, authority is the home lock or an approved running cover and nothing
else. When it is disarmed, nothing narrows at all — the device does not *grant* a
branch either, it simply stops being contradicted. Cross-branch trusted-device use
is valid in both.

## AW-R15 — the client's words are not the server's reason

The Clinic App reported **"sesi perangkat tidak dapat dibuat"**. That string names
no cause, and reading it as a device or transport fault would have been wrong: the
server had recorded `active_session_elsewhere`.

Always resolve a refusal from `sys_audit_logs`, the login ticket and the
challenge record, correlated by time window. And note the shape of the evidence —
the rejection row carried **`performed_by = NULL`** while the preceding request
row carried the user id, so a per-user audit query alone would have missed the
refusal entirely. Compare with the same trap recorded at rule 156 for
"tidak dapat menghubungi server".

## AW-R16 — arming makes a wrong home lock visible immediately

A home lock that disagrees with where a doctor actually works is inert and
invisible until the branch lock arms, and narrowing from that instant. The
2026-09-17 proof subject is the live example: locked to TLK1 while every observed
signal — her visits, her online context, her proven tablet — is ATG3.

**How it was actually resolved is the lesson.** The available fixes were a governed
transfer, an approved cover, or disarming the capability. The owner chose the
third, because the mismatch was not one doctor's data being wrong — it was the
*model* being wrong: the business wants doctors to work at any branch on a
registered device, which no amount of per-doctor transfers expresses.

So: when arming surfaces a contradiction, ask whether the data is wrong or the
model is, **before** writing transfers. And re-check the home-branch matrix against
real working location before any future re-arm, never after the first narrowed
clinic.

## AW-R17 — a model is not enforced until every subject is covered

The stated model has two halves: *work at any branch*, and *only on a registered
device of that branch*. On 2026-09-17 the first became true for all 15 doctors and
the second binds only the 3-doctor cohort `[9,15,18]` — the other 12 can still log
in by browser with no device at all.

Making the device requirement fleet-wide **is Half B**, which is blocked on real
hardware and on attestations that are untrue today. Never report a half-enforced
model as enforced, and never close the gap by signing an attestation to make a
gate green.
